WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.375
Deliberate practice builds skill through specific goals, focused effort, feedback, correction, repetition, variation, and measurement.

2
00:00:06.375 --> 00:00:11.475
It is different from ordinary repetition because every attempt has a purpose.

3
00:00:11.475 --> 00:00:21.676
It is different from performance because practice creates space to isolate weak points and improve them before the learner returns to the full task.

4
00:00:21.676 --> 00:00:28.051
Strong practice systems target the right subskill and operate near the edge of current ability.

5
00:00:28.051 --> 00:00:33.576
They use feedback to reveal the gap between current performance and desired performance.

6
00:00:33.576 --> 00:00:36.976
They correct errors before those errors become habits.

7
00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:44.202
They repeat the important behavior enough to build fluency and vary the conditions enough to support transfer.

8
00:00:44.202 --> 00:00:47.602
Measurement helps the learner avoid guessing about progress.

9
00:00:47.602 --> 00:00:52.702
Baselines, rubrics, examples, logs, performance data, and decision records all provide evidence.

10
00:00:52.702 --> 00:00:57.377
The question is not whether practice felt intense or consumed time.

11
00:00:57.377 --> 00:01:05.878
The question is whether real capability improved and whether the learner is better prepared to perform when the work matters.

12
00:01:05.878 --> 00:01:10.553
The final takeaway is that deliberate practice is structured, sustainable improvement.

13
00:01:10.553 --> 00:01:20.753
It asks learners to choose the skill clearly, practice with attention, seek useful feedback, correct deliberately, and keep the system realistic enough to continue.

14
00:01:20.753 --> 00:01:24.578
The goal is reliable skill growth, not endless strain.

15
00:01:24.578 --> 00:01:30.953
With the right loop, focused effort becomes capability that can travel back into real work.
